{"benchmark_id": "bmk-v1-002", "category": "ordinary_tutorial", "context_hash": "a655fb123c0f4bf33a28144790347b3b9d2670bb98479d0562aba83ef177fbf4", "msgctxt": "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|and what Gmsh is not so good at|paragraph|3587489ab996a1a52601d4c7ed0358ad8b20c5e27ef2274f73645bde8164877c", "msgid": "While Gmsh can create and manipulate geometries interactively, it is in\nno way a full-fledged replacement for an advanced CAD system.", "msgid_sha256": "3587489ab996a1a52601d4c7ed0358ad8b20c5e27ef2274f73645bde8164877c", "node": "and what Gmsh is not so good at", "protected_hash": "4f53cda18c2baa0c0354bb5f9a3ecbe5ed12ab4d8e11ba873c2f11161202b945", "protected_values": [], "reference_translation": "", "reference_translation_status": "pending", "role": "paragraph", "schema_version": 1, "selection_method": "sha256-stratified-v1", "selection_rank": "00cd43cb56c598f7f8c594b68e813f0eeeab77cfe42985c019c2e427032cd702", "semantic_key": "3587489ab996a1a52601d4c7ed0358ad8b20c5e27ef2274f73645bde8164877c", "source_file": "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi", "source_hash": "2920c082129a349b092c54aa92c4694f70277fe1b3a8a164a5544e26cc2ac933", "structure_path": "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi::and what Gmsh is not so good at", "subcategory": "prose", "unit_id": "8ad6aa5f8d20f2bd18df6aee394f22245f310881fe2286499b98079eabe2ae48"}
{"benchmark_id": "bmk-v1-003", "category": "ordinary_tutorial", "context_hash": "5c11f568dc46c8aaaa601bedefb63488f68ac4fb65c2858e5fefd2ebae3fbe5f", "msgctxt": "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Geometry module|paragraph|each volume must possess a unique tag.#2", "msgid": "Model entities can be manipulated and transformed in a variety of ways\nwithin the geometry module, but operations are always performed directly\nwithin their respective CAD kernels. As explained above, there is no\ncommon internal geometrical representation: rather, Gmsh directly\nperforms the operations (translation, rotation, intersection, union,\nfragments, ...) on the native geometrical representation using each CAD\nkernel's own API.  In the same philosophy, models can be imported in the\ngeometry module through each CAD kernel's own import mechanisms. For\nexample, by default Gmsh imports STEP and IGES files through\nOpenCASCADE, which will lead to the creation of model entities with an\ninternal OpenCASCADE representation. Models represented with the\nbuilt-in CAD kernel can be serialized to disk by exporting them as\n@file{.geo_unrolled} files, while models contructed with the OpenCASCADE\nkernel can be serialized as @file{.brep} or @file{.xao} files.", "msgid_sha256": "22f9dca909b6902d9c3b1e9fcdddb0f1cece75b2f0d627ae30b95a30329bc53d", "node": "Geometry module", "protected_hash": "f988a5ac5436249b8200d7bd50ccc797c435b01812f8fc817eeca2c2bdbade95", "protected_values": ["@file{.geo_unrolled}", "@file{.brep}", "@file{.xao}"], "reference_translation": "", "reference_translation_status": "pending", "role": "paragraph", "schema_version": 1, "selection_method": "sha256-stratified-v1", "selection_rank": "01ee9d3691aa5bfa673508208c17ceb489b548cfc3b5136b018d29f2c89733cd", "semantic_key": "each volume must possess a unique tag.#2", "source_file": "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi", "source_hash": "60878ef4766f22ee109f1b2716dea4a85bac260b556078d455dd2371bb83fd47", "structure_path": "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi::Geometry module", "subcategory": "prose", "unit_id": "eb8104623b67902bf1aa12614278b171d2c9d75f6dd5d2869e50ca2a84825a69"}
